WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.369
A waypoint of Indian Ocean trade for two thousand years.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.800
A Greek sailing guide of the first century, the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.812
describes Socotra as a trading post for tortoiseshell, aloes and dragon's blood.

00:00:14.812 --> 00:00:17.506
Marco Polo reported Christians living here.

00:00:17.506 --> 00:00:23.145
In 1507 a Portuguese fleet under Tristão da Cunha, the same navigator who sighted Tristan,

00:00:23.145 --> 00:00:26.027
captured its fort, but left a few years later.

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:32.483
Sailors who sheltered in Hoq Cave left inscriptions in Indian Brahmi, Greek, Aramaic,

00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:37.146
South Arabian and other scripts, mostly from the first to sixth centuries.

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:42.697
Western scientific expeditions arrived in the nineteenth century.

00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:47.627
Henry Ogg Forbes photographed Hadibo and the Haggeher mountains in 1898.

00:00:48.727 --> 00:00:55.072
Later the island was ruled by the Mahra sultans, became a British protectorate in 1886,

00:00:55.072 --> 00:00:59.228
and joined South Yemen in 1967 and unified Yemen in 1990.

00:00:59.228 --> 00:01:05.063
Since the civil war, the United Arab Emirates has built influence on the island,

00:01:05.063 --> 00:01:08.417
and in 2020 a UAE-backed council took control.
